{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791733741731,"sessionID":"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_DfUKMnoDuEBrZOdRXDhw8970","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/archive/medical_policies/brief.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/archive/medical_policies/brief.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Brief: policies\n2: \n3: type: feature\n4: id: medical\n5: branch: feature/medical_policies\n6: date: 2026-10-10\n7: author: Leander Muskalla\n8: \n9: ## What\n10: \n11: Add a `policies/` directory to the defaults catalog with a small set of\n12: policy files (`policies/<name>.md`). The catalog today has `agents/`,\n13: `skills/`, `mcp/` and `prompts/` — but no `policies/` at all.\n14: \n15: ## Background: what a policy is\n16: \n17: A manigot **policy** is a plain-markdown file, one rule per file, injected\n18: **unconditionally** into every session's project context. Unlike a skill —\n19: which the agent loads only when it judges the one-line description relevant —\n20: a policy is always in effect. Two tiers:\n21: \n22: - **Global** — the catalog, installed with `mg defaults install policies/<name>`\n23:   into the user data dir, active in **every project** on the machine.\n24: - **Project** — committed at `<project>/docs/policies/<name>.md`, active only\n25:   there (and, planned, vendorable from the catalog per project).\n26: \n27: See manigot's `internal/session/policies.go` and the `Policies` bullet in its\n28: `AGENTS.md` for the mechanism.\n29: \n30: ## The skills-vs-policies line (read before writing anything)\n31: \n32: The catalog already carries deep code-quality and security *knowledge* as\n33: skills: `go-code-review`, `typescript-guidelines`, `python-guidelines`,\n34: `laravel-best-practices`, `security-best-practices`, `test-driven-development`\n35: and ~70 more. That is the right home for that content, and it must not be\n36: duplicated here.\n37: \n38: The distinction is **not topic** — skills and policies both touch quality,\n39: security and process. It is **trigger and depth**:\n40: \n41: - **Skill** = on-demand depth. A body of knowledge, checklist or workflow,\n42:   loaded when the model judges it relevant. Because it only fires when\n43:   relevant, a catalog skill may be broad and stack-specific.\n44: - **Policy** = always-on constraint. A short rule that must hold whether or\n45:   not the model considers it relevant. Because a catalog policy is global and\n46:   unconditional, it must be **universal**.\n47: \n48: Decision rule for any proposed policy:\n49: \n50: 1. Is it true for every project, every language, every task? If not, it is not\n51:    a catalog policy.\n52: 2. Is it a short \"always/never\" constraint rather than advice or a checklist?\n53:    If it needs more than a few lines, it is a skill.\n54: 3. Does it avoid restating a skill, or the core agents' own instructions\n55:    (`agents/developer.md` etc.)? If it just repeats one, don't add it.\n56: 4. Is it *situational* (\"when writing Go, …\")? Then it is a **skill**, or a\n57:    **project** policy — never a global catalog policy.\n58: \n59: ## Proposed starter set\n60: \n61: Small on purpose — quality over quantity. The names/content are a starting\n62: suggestion; the criteria above are the real spec.\n63: \n64: - `secrets-hygiene` — never commit, log or print secrets, tokens, keys or\n65:   credentials; read them from env/config, never hardcode.\n66: - `no-debug-leftovers` — leave no debug output, commented-out code, dead code,\n67:   or stray TODO (without a tracked issue) behind.\n68: - `smallest-change` — make the smallest change that satisfies the task; don't\n69:   refactor or tidy adjacent code, and don't expand scope.\n70: - `ask-before-adding-dependencies` — don't add a dependency without explicit\n71:   approval.\n72: - `keep-it-committed` — leave no uncommitted work behind.\n73: \n74: ## Format\n75: \n76: - Path: `policies/<name>.md`. One policy per file; the **filename is the\n77:   identity** — no frontmatter, no id field.\n78: - Plain markdown body: a one-line heading + the rule + at most a line or two\n79:   of why. Keep each file short.\n80: - Files are read verbatim and concatenated (sorted by filename) into the\n81:   session context — no frontmatter, no templating.\n82: - Keep the directory to policy files only; the catalog scan ignores hidden\n83:   files, `README`, and symlinks.\n84: \n85: ## Non-goals\n86: \n87: - No stack- or language-specific rules (those are skills, or per-project\n88:   policies).\n89: - No long documents, checklists or workflows (those are skills).\n90: - No rule that merely repeats an existing skill or the core agent prompts.\n\n(End of file - total 90 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Brief: policies\n\ntype: feature\nid: medical\nbranch: feature/medical_policies\ndate: 2026-10-10\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\n\n## What\n\nAdd a `policies/` directory to the defaults catalog with a small set of\npolicy files (`policies/<name>.md`). The catalog today has `agents/`,\n`skills/`, `mcp/` and `prompts/` — but no `policies/` at all.\n\n## Background: what a policy is\n\nA manigot **policy** is a plain-markdown file, one rule per file, injected\n**unconditionally** into every session's project context. Unlike a skill —\nwhich the agent loads only when it judges the one-line description relevant —\na policy is always in effect. Two tiers:","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/jobs/archive/medical_policies/brief.md","text":"# Brief: policies\n\ntype: feature\nid: medical\nbranch: feature/medical_policies\ndate: 2026-10-10\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\n\n## What\n\nAdd a `policies/` directory to the defaults catalog with a small set of\npolicy files (`policies/<name>.md`). The catalog today has `agents/`,\n`skills/`, `mcp/` and `prompts/` — but no `policies/` at all.\n\n## Background: what a policy is\n\nA manigot **policy** is a plain-markdown file, one rule per file, injected\n**unconditionally** into every session's project context. Unlike a skill —\nwhich the agent loads only when it judges the one-line description relevant —\na policy is always in effect. Two tiers:\n\n- **Global** — the catalog, installed with `mg defaults install policies/<name>`\n  into the user data dir, active in **every project** on the machine.\n- **Project** — committed at `<project>/docs/policies/<name>.md`, active only\n  there (and, planned, vendorable from the catalog per project).\n\nSee manigot's `internal/session/policies.go` and the `Policies` bullet in its\n`AGENTS.md` for the mechanism.\n\n## The skills-vs-policies line (read before writing anything)\n\nThe catalog already carries deep code-quality and security *knowledge* as\nskills: `go-code-review`, `typescript-guidelines`, `python-guidelines`,\n`laravel-best-practices`, `security-best-practices`, `test-driven-development`\nand ~70 more. That is the right home for that content, and it must not be\nduplicated here.\n\nThe distinction is **not topic** — skills and policies both touch quality,\nsecurity and process. It is **trigger and depth**:\n\n- **Skill** = on-demand depth. A body of knowledge, checklist or workflow,\n  loaded when the model judges it relevant. Because it only fires when\n  relevant, a catalog skill may be broad and stack-specific.\n- **Policy** = always-on constraint. A short rule that must hold whether or\n  not the model considers it relevant. Because a catalog policy is global and\n  unconditional, it must be **universal**.\n\nDecision rule for any proposed policy:\n\n1. Is it true for every project, every language, every task? If not, it is not\n   a catalog policy.\n2. Is it a short \"always/never\" constraint rather than advice or a checklist?\n   If it needs more than a few lines, it is a skill.\n3. Does it avoid restating a skill, or the core agents' own instructions\n   (`agents/developer.md` etc.)? If it just repeats one, don't add it.\n4. Is it *situational* (\"when writing Go, …\")? Then it is a **skill**, or a\n   **project** policy — never a global catalog policy.\n\n## Proposed starter set\n\nSmall on purpose — quality over quantity. The names/content are a starting\nsuggestion; the criteria above are the real spec.\n\n- `secrets-hygiene` — never commit, log or print secrets, tokens, keys or\n  credentials; read them from env/config, never hardcode.\n- `no-debug-leftovers` — leave no debug output, commented-out code, dead code,\n  or stray TODO (without a tracked issue) behind.\n- `smallest-change` — make the smallest change that satisfies the task; don't\n  refactor or tidy adjacent code, and don't expand scope.\n- `ask-before-adding-dependencies` — don't add a dependency without explicit\n  approval.\n- `keep-it-committed` — leave no uncommitted work behind.\n\n## Format\n\n- Path: `policies/<name>.md`. One policy per file; the **filename is the\n  identity** — no frontmatter, no id field.\n- Plain markdown body: a one-line heading + the rule + at most a line or two\n  of why. Keep each file short.\n- Files are read verbatim and concatenated (sorted by filename) into the\n  session context — no frontmatter, no templating.\n- Keep the directory to policy files only; the catalog scan ignores hidden\n  files, `README`, and symlinks.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- No stack- or language-specific rules (those are skills, or per-project\n  policies).\n- No long documents, checklists or workflows (those are skills).\n- No rule that merely repeats an existing skill or the core agent prompts.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":90,"totalLines":90,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/jobs/archive/medical_policies/brief.md","time":{"start":1791733741694,"end":1791733741721}},"id":"prt_12ba73807001GlZnsrm3kLx0Su","sessionID":"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB","messageID":"msg_12ba72ff8001r8cY9L3OOAsABb"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791733741825,"sessionID":"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_PjV9Fc3IhuAyDuO6yIEx9638","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/jobs/archive/medical_policies/verdict.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/archive/medical_policies/verdict.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Verdict: policies\n2: \n3: id: medical\n4: reviewer: reviewer\n5: date: 2026-10-10\n6: \n7: <!-- Produced by @reviewer and/or @security after implementation. -->\n8: \n9: ## Review\n10: \n11: Branch: `feature/medical_policies` (matches brief). Base: `main` (no\n12: `.manigot/manigot.json` present → fallback). Reviewed via `git diff main...HEAD`.\n13: \n14: TASK-1: PASS\n15: notes: `policies/secrets-hygiene.md` present (5 lines: one-line heading +\n16: rule + 2 lines why). Universal, no frontmatter, filename-only identity.\n17: Matches brief wording (never commit/log/print/hardcode secrets; read from\n18: env/config). No always-on rule existed before (only role checklists in\n19: `agents/security.md:70,88,106` and a passing mention in `agents/architect.md`),\n20: so it adds coverage rather than restating an always-on constraint.\n21: \n22: TASK-2: PASS\n23: notes: `policies/no-debug-leftovers.md` present, short and universal.\n24: Judgement call verified: the wording closely mirrors `agents/quality.md:92`\n25: (\"no dead code, no commented-out code, no debug output, no TODO comments left\n26: without a tracking issue\"). Criterion 3 warns against restating a core agent's\n27: instructions, but that line is role-scoped to the on-demand `quality` review\n28: agent and never reaches other sessions; promoting it to an always-on rule is\n29: genuinely broader. The brief itself lists `no-debug-leftovers` in the proposed\n30: starter set, so keeping it is within the intended latitude. Acceptable.\n31: \n32: TASK-3: PASS\n33: notes: `policies/smallest-change.md` was created (commit 26a6159) then removed\n34: by the TASK-6 audit (commit cd9f132); it is absent from the final diff. This is\n35: the correct outcome and explicitly anticipated by both the brief and tasks.md\n36: (\"the final set may be fewer than five\"). Verified the removal rationale:\n37: `prompts/meta.md:10-11,15` already imposes this session-wide (\"never silently\n38: expand a task\", \"Don't do things you're not asked to do\"), so a catalog policy\n39: would be a pure restatement per criterion 3. No deliverable is missing.\n40: \n41: TASK-4: PASS\n42: notes: `policies/ask-before-adding-dependencies.md` present, short, framed\n43: session-wide. Only prior coverage is `agents/developer.md:51` (\"Do not install\n44: packages without asking first\"), which is developer-role-only; the policy adds\n45: all-session scope. Passes the dedupe check.\n46: \n47: TASK-5: PASS\n48: notes: `policies/keep-it-committed.md` present, short, universal. Prior\n49: coverage is developer-role-only commit steps in `agents/developer.md`;\n50: `prompts/meta.md` does not cover committing. Adds scope. The\n51: implementation.md caveat (read-only agents have nothing to commit) is honest\n52: and does not undermine the rule.\n53: \n54: TASK-6: PASS\n55: notes: Audit performed and fully recorded in `implementation.md`. Dropped\n56: exactly one file (`smallest-change`) with per-file rationale; kept four. Every\n57: dedupe claim spot-checked against the actual repo and confirmed\n58: (`prompts/meta.md`, `agents/quality.md:92`, `agents/security.md`, \n59: `agents/developer.md:51`). No frontmatter, `README`, hidden files or symlinks\n60: in `policies/` (verified: directory contains exactly the four `.md` files).\n61: \n62: Scope: clean. `git diff main...HEAD --name-only` shows only the four\n63: `policies/*.md` files plus this job's own `docs/jobs/medical_policies/`\n64: artifacts (brief/tasks/implementation/verdict/session.log/tokens.json). No\n65: changes to `agents/`, `skills/`, `mcp/`, `prompts/` or anything else. The\n66: `session.log`/`tokens.json` changes are framework bookkeeping, not developer\n67: edits — not a finding.\n68: \n69: ## Security\n70: \n71: No security findings. `secrets-hygiene.md` is itself a security policy and is\n72: phrased correctly (keep secrets out of source, diffs, command output, error\n73: messages). No secret material introduced.\n74: \n75: ## Overall\n76: \n77: APPROVED\n78: \n79: All six tasks are satisfied and match the brief's format and decision\n80: criteria. Nothing must change before merge. Non-blocking observations only:\n81: \n82: - The TASK-6 dedupe audit was necessarily limited to the content in this\n83:   checkout; the brief's referenced `internal/session/policies.go`, `AGENTS.md`\n84:   and \"~70 more\" skills are not present here. `implementation.md` records this\n85:   caveat, which is the correct handling.\n\n(End of file - total 85 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Verdict: policies\n\nid: medical\nreviewer: reviewer\ndate: 2026-10-10\n\n<!-- Produced by @reviewer and/or @security after implementation. -->\n\n## Review\n\nBranch: `feature/medical_policies` (matches brief). Base: `main` (no\n`.manigot/manigot.json` present → fallback). Reviewed via `git diff main...HEAD`.\n\nTASK-1: PASS\nnotes: `policies/secrets-hygiene.md` present (5 lines: one-line heading +\nrule + 2 lines why). Universal, no frontmatter, filename-only identity.\nMatches brief wording (never commit/log/print/hardcode secrets; read from\nenv/config). No always-on rule existed before (only role checklists in\n`agents/security.md:70,88,106` and a passing mention in `agents/architect.md`),\nso it adds coverage rather than restating an always-on constraint.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/jobs/archive/medical_policies/verdict.md","text":"# Verdict: policies\n\nid: medical\nreviewer: reviewer\ndate: 2026-10-10\n\n<!-- Produced by @reviewer and/or @security after implementation. -->\n\n## Review\n\nB