nothing uncommitted, including files earlier agents\\n65: left behind (e.g. the analyst's `tasks.md`).\\n66: \\n67: **Step 4 — Repeat** for the next task.\\n68: \\n69: ## After all tasks are done\\n\\n(Showing lines 40-69 of 129. Use offset=70 to continue.)\\n</content>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"3. Check which branch you are on: `git branch --show-current` — the mounted workspace is the job's own worktree, always on the job branch, so no `git checkout` is needed\\n4. Compare against the `branch:` field in `brief.md`\\n5. If they differ, stop and report back — do not implement on the wrong branch\\n\\n## For each task, follow these steps in order\\n\\n**Step 1 — Read** the relevant files before touching anything.\\n\\n**Step 2 — Implement** exactly what the task requires. Nothing more.\\n- Make the smallest change that correctly fulfills the task\\n- Do not refactor adjacent code unless the task explicitly requires it\\n- Do not install packages without asking first\\n- If the task turns out to be more complex than described: stop and report back, do not expand scope\\n\\n**Step 3 — Commit** once the task is complete. One commit per task is the\\nrecommended pattern, but the exact message format is not required — the whole\\nbranch is squashed into a single commit at `mg done`, so per-task commit\\nhygiene is not a review criterion.\\n```\\ngit add -A\",\"truncated\":true,\"loaded\":[],\"display\":{\"type\":\"file\",\"path\":\"/workspace/agents/developer.md\",\"text\":\"3. Check which branch you are on: `git branch --show-current` — the mounted workspace is the job's own worktree, always on the job branch, so no `git checkout` is needed\\n4. Compare against the `branch:` field in `brief.md`\\n5. If they differ, stop and report back — do not implement on the wrong branch\\n\\n## For each task, follow these steps in order\\n\\n**Step 1 — Read** the relevant files before touching anything.\\n\\n**Step 2 — Implement** exactly what the task requires. Nothing more.\\n- Make the smallest change that correctly fulfills the task\\n- Do not refactor adjacent code unless the task explicitly requires it\\n- Do not install packages without asking first\\n- If the task turns out to be more complex than described: stop and report back, do not expand scope\\n\\n**Step 3 — Commit** once the task is complete. One commit per task is the\\nrecommended pattern, but the exact message format is not required — the whole\\nbranch is squashed into a single commit at `mg done`, so per-task commit\\nhygiene is not a review criterion.\\n```\\ngit add -A\\ngit commit -m \\\"short description\\\"\\n```\\n\\nA task should be committed before moving to the next one. When you finish,\\nleave the worktree clean: nothing uncommitted, including files earlier agents\\nleft behind (e.g. the analyst's `tasks.md`).\\n\\n**Step 4 — Repeat** for the next task.\\n\\n## After all tasks are done\",\"lineStart\":40,\"lineEnd\":69,\"totalLines\":129,\"truncated\":true}},\"title\":\"agents/developer.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1791733763180,\"end\":1791733763211}},\"id\":\"prt_12ba78bd7001zrr7YAzcdYXKsJ\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba76aab001tTYmWbtP6CjN2d\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1791733763293,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba78cca001nz00WbtnQ6VV8i\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"snapshot\":\"\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba76aab001tTYmWbtP6CjN2d\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":42084,\"input\":2276,\"output\":182,\"reasoning\":970,\"cache\":{\"write\":0,\"read\":38656}},\"cost\":0.001148568}}\n+{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1791733764484,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba79181001VCAsvvsKrceIi7\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba78cd7001pIoFXm6ssacJlY\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"snapshot\":\"\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1791733774569,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"write\",\"callID\":\"call_00_3k2Z5AQR0eO9LNxTSWF45089\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/tasks.md\",\"content\":\"# Tasks: new policy: playwright only if necessary\\n\\nid: prior\\nanalyst: analyst\\ndate: 2026-10-11\\n\\n<!-- Produced by @analyst from brief.md. -->\\n\\n## Overview\\n\\nAdd one new global catalog policy under `policies/` capturing the brief's rule:\\nbrowser automation (Playwright, and the `shot` render path that drives it) is\\nexpensive, so it should be reached for only when it is genuinely necessary or\\nhelpful — static analysis and cheaper verification come first. This is a\\ncontent-only change: a single plain-markdown policy file, no frontmatter,\\nfollowing the format the `medical_policies` job established (one-line heading +\\nthe rule + at most a line or two of why; the filename is the identity).\\n\\nScope boundaries confirmed from the brief:\\n- The deliverable is a **global catalog policy** at `policies/<name>.md` — the\\n  brief says \\\"create a new policy\\\", and this repo's source-of-truth policy\\n  directory is the top-level `policies/` (not `docs/policies/`, which is the\\n  per-project tier described in `docs/AGENTS.md`).\\n- Do **not** touch `agents/`, `skills/`, `mcp/`, `prompts/`, or anything under\\n  `docs/` other than this job's own files.\\n- Do **not** add a `README`, hidden files, or symlinks inside `policies/` — keep\\n  the directory to policy files only.\\n- Keep the file short: heading + rule + at most a line or two of why.\\n\\n## Framing note for @developer (important, not a blocker)\\n\\nThe `medical_policies` brief's decision criteria for a global catalog policy\\nare the real format spec here: a policy must be **universal** (true for every\\nproject/language/task) and must **not be situational or tool-specific** — if a\\nrule is \\\"when doing X, …\\\", it belongs in a **skill** or a **project** policy,\\nnever a global catalog one. A rule named purely after one tool (`playwright`)\\nreads as situational and would fail criterion 1/4.\\n\\nRecommended resolution: frame the policy around the **universal principle** —\\nprefer the cheapest sufficient verification; reserve slow/heavyweight tooling\\n(browser automation such as Playwright, or `shot` renders) for when static\\nanalysis, reading the code, or lighter tests genuinely cannot answer the\\nquestion — and name Playwright as the concrete motivating example rather than\\nthe whole subject. Suggested filename: `policies/playwright-only-if-necessary.md`\\n(the filename is the policy's identity and should stay recognisable).\\n\\nKnown tension to record (do **not** silently expand scope): `agents/developer.md`\\nlines ~107-129 instruct the developer to verify UI changes with `shot`, and\\n`agents/tester.md` line ~96 points at the `webapp-testing` skill (Playwright).\\nThe policy must **complement** those role instructions, not contradict them;\\nword it so it governs *when* to reach for the browser, leaving the existing\\n\\\"verify rendered UI work\\\" guidance intact. If a genuine contradiction cannot be\\navoided without editing an agent file, stop and flag it in `implementation.md`\\nrather than editing `agents/` (that is out of scope for this brief).\\n\\n## Task breakdown\\n\\n### TASK-1 — Author `policies/playwright-only-if-necessary.md`\\n\\n- **Description**: Create the global policy file carrying the brief's rule —\\n  browser automation / Playwright is a costly verification path, so use it only\\n  when it is strictly necessary or genuinely helpful; prefer static analysis and\\n  cheaper checks first. Write it as a short, universal, session-wide always/\\n  never constraint (heading + rule + a line of why), tool-neutral in spirit with\\n  Playwright named only as the motivating example.\\n- **Files**: `policies/playwright-only-if-necessary.md` (new)\\n- **Depends**: none\\n- **Risk**: low — additive markdown file only; the only judgement call is the\\n  universal framing (see note above), not any code path.\\n\\n### TASK-2 — Audit the new policy against the existing catalog\\n\\n- **Description**: Check the new file against the `medical_policies` criteria\\n  and the catalog content that actually exists here: shorten wording if it\\n  exceeds a short rule, and confirm it (a) is universal, (b) adds scope rather\\n  than restating the `webapp-testing`/`frontend-design` skills or\\n  `prompts/meta.md`, and (c) does not contradict `agents/developer.md` (the\\n  `shot` render guidance) or `agents/tester.md` (Playwright via\\n  `webapp-testing`). Adjust the phrasing to resolve any conflict within the\\n  policy file; record the dedupe/consistency reasoning and any decision that\\n  cannot be made in-file in `implementation.md`.\\n- **Files**: `policies/playwright-only-if-necessary.md` (may be edited);\\n  `implementation.md` (record the outcome)\\n- **Depends**: TASK-1\\n- **Risk**: medium — a judgement-based pass that can change or trim the\\n  delivered wording, so its result should be reviewer-checked against the\\n  brief's criteria and the \\\"add scope, don't restate\\\" rule.\\n\\n## Notes / open questions\\n\\n- The brief's `Why`, `Out of scope`, and `Notes` sections are empty; the What is\\n  specific enough to proceed. No human decision was required. The main open\\n  design point — universal-principle framing vs. a Playwright-named rule — is\\n  resolved in favour of the universal framing above, because a situational rule\\n  does not qualify as a global catalog policy; if the reviewer disagrees, the\\n  alternative is a `docs/policies/` project rule or a skill, which is a larger\\n  decision than this brief describes.\\n- This repo has no build/test tooling; verification is content review against\\n  the policy format and criteria, not `go test`/CI.\\n- If a follow-up is ever wanted (e.g. softening `agents/developer.md`'s\\n  unconditional \\\"verify UI with shot\\\" wording), it is out of scope here and\\n  should be tracked as its own job.\\n\"},\"output\":\"Wrote file successfully.\",\"metadata\":{\"diagnostics\":{},\"filepath\":\"/workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/tasks.md\",\"exists\":true,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/tasks.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1791733774544,\"end\":1791733774563}},\"id\":\"prt_12ba79b83001l65PxXaCWNKehK\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba78cd7001pIoFXm6ssacJlY\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1791733774637,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba7b91d001JTkqUSSfHUfuT2\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"snapshot\":\"\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba78cd7001pIoFXm6ssacJlY\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":44239,\"input\":550,\"output\":1438,\"reasoning\":267,\"cache\":{\"write\":0,\"read\":41984}},\"cost\":0.001231452}}\n+{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1791733775804,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba7bdb9001rh5ObGbjcnKDZB\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7b92a001ni4rn5R9de6zNm\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"snapshot\":\"\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"text\",\"timestamp\":1791733780401,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba7c115001a9kkY8tSya2gug\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7b92a001ni4rn5R9de6zNm\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Done. I read `brief.md`, verified the branch, and wrote the task list to `tasks.md`.\\n\\n## Branch check\\n`brief.md` says `branch: feature/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary`; the worktree HEAD (`/workspace/.git` → worktree `.../prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/HEAD`) is `refs/heads/feature/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary`. They match — proceeding.\\n\\n## What I found\\n- This is the manigot-catalog content repo. Policies live in the top-level `policies/` (not `docs/policies/`, which is the per-project tier). Existing files: `ask-before-adding-dependencies.md`, `keep-it-committed.md`, `no-debug-leftovers.md`, `secrets-hygiene.md`.\\n- The archived `docs/jobs/archive/medical_policies/` job is the format precedent: plain markdown, no frontmatter, filename is identity, heading + rule + a line of why, and four decision criteria (universal, short always/never, no restating skills/agents, not situational).\\n- Relevant existing content the new policy must not contradict: `agents/developer.md` (~107–129) tells the developer to verify UI with `shot`; `agents/tester.md` (~96) points at the `webapp-testing` skill (Playwright); plus the `webapp-testing`/`frontend-design` skills and `prompts/meta.md`.\\n\\n## Task list (written to `tasks.md`)\\n\\n- **TASK-1 — Author `policies/playwright-only-if-necessary.md`.** Create the short, universal always-on policy: browser automation/Playwright is costly, so use it only when strictly necessary/helpful; prefer static analysis and cheaper checks. *Files:* new policy file. *Depends:* none. *Risk:* low — additive markdown; only judgement call is framing.\\n- **TASK-2 — Audit the new policy against the existing catalog.** Check it against the `medical_policies` criteria and existing agent/skill/meta-prompt content, shorten if needed, confirm it adds scope and does not contradict `developer.md`/`tester.md`, and record the reasoning in `implementation.md`. *Files:* policy file (may be edited), `implementation.md`. *Depends:* TASK-1. *Risk:* medium — judgement-based, can change/trim the wording.\\n\\n## Two things I flagged rather than guessed\\n1. **Framing vs. the criteria:** a rule named purely after one tool reads as *situational*, which the precedent says disqualifies a *global* policy. I resolved this conservatively toward the universal principle (prefer the cheapest sufficient verification; name Playwright as the example) and noted the alternative (a `docs/policies/` project rule or a skill) if the reviewer prefers it.\\n2. **Tension with `agents/developer.md`:** its unconditional \\\"verify UI changes with `shot`\\\" guidance conflicts with \\\"only use Playwright if necessary.\\\" I scoped the fix to wording inside the policy and explicitly kept editing `agents/` out of scope, to be flagged if it can't be reconciled in-file.\\n\\nThe brief's `Why`/`Out of scope`/`Notes` are empty, but the What is specific enough to proceed — no human decision needed. I did not commit (read-only agent).\",\"time\":{\"start\":1791733776661,\"end\":1791733780395}}}\n+{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1791733780436,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba7cfc2001cJoL6W2iC5TVvj\",\"reason\":\"stop\",\"snapshot\":\"\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7b92a001ni4rn5R9de6zNm\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":45039,\"input\":223,\"output\":735,\"reasoning\":49,\"cache\":{\"write\":0,\"read\":44032}},\"cost\":0.000635946}}\n+\n+=== 2026-10-11T17:49:40+02:00 developer (attempt 1) ===\n+{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1791733788280,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba7ee7300161OYW6EwiI9Jp3\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7e0dd001Zt6uQvxOzblyTf\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"snapshot\":\"\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1791733789690,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"call_00_GyLEKTlHCf2TyinCBxmZ6708\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"git branch --show-current && echo \\\"---\\\" && git status --short && echo \\\"---\\\" && ls /workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary\"},\"output\":\"feature/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary\\n---\\n M docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/session.log\\n?? docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/tokens.json\\n---\\nbrief.md\\nimplementation.md\\nsession.log\\ntasks.md\\ntokens.json\\nverdict.md\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"feature/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary\\n---\\n M docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/session.log\\n?? docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/tokens.json\\n---\\nbrief.md\\nimplementation.md\\nsession.log\\ntasks.md\\ntokens.json\\nverdict.md\\n\",\"exit\":0,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"git branch --show-current && echo \\\"---\\\" && git status --short && echo \\\"---\\\" && ls /workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary\",\"time\":{\"start\":1791733789481,\"end\":1791733789653}},\"id\":\"prt_12ba7f287001VSo82t9dvmb05P\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7e0dd001Zt6uQvxOzblyTf\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1791733789785,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba7f440001Al6yyfAF3r5GCq\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"snapshot\":\"\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7e0dd001Zt6uQvxOzblyTf\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":19438,\"input\":19347,\"output\":75,\"reasoning\":16,\"cache\":{\"write\":0,\"read\":0}},\"cost\":0.00295665}}\n+{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1791733791004,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba7f917001yS2YJteumnLZuz\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7f450001RWwpADlrw5uoec\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"snapshot\":\"\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1791733791892,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_00_qg9ldgBgjodg434GUr8y6009\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/brief.md\"},\"output\":\"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/brief.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: # Brief: new policy: playwright only if necessary\\n2: \\n3: type: feature\\n4: id: prior\\n5: branch: feature/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary\\n6: date: 2026-10-11\\n7: author: Leander Muskalla\\n8: \\n9: ## What\\n10: \\n11: Let's create a new policy: only use playwright if strictly helpful. The agents get stuck a long time trying to playwright every possible thing instead of using easy paths of static analysis. Playwright can be helpful, but it costs and should only be used if really necessary/helpful.\\n12: \\n13: ## Why\\n14: \\n15: <!-- Why does this need to exist? What problem does it solve for the user? -->\\n16: \\n17: ## Out of scope\\n18: \\n19: <!-- What are we explicitly NOT doing in this job? -->\\n20: \\n21: ## Notes\\n22: \\n23: <!-- Anything the analyst or developer should know before starting. -->\\n\\n(End of file - total 23 lines)\\n</content>\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nInstructions from: /workspace/docs/AGENTS.md\\n# manigot-catalog\\n\\n<!--\\nThis is your project context, loaded by the agent at the start of every session.\\nmanigot is vendor-agnostic: it runs Claude Code or OpenCode against the same\\nproject (`mg --profile claude-pro` vs `mg --profile zai`/`--profile\\nopencode-go`/`--profile opencode-zen`/`--profile opencode-zen-free`/`--profile\\ndeepseek-direct`), and this one file serves both — manigot mounts it read-only\\nwherever the selected tool looks for it\\n(/workspace/AGENTS.md for OpenCode, /workspace/.claude/CLAUDE.md for Claude\\nCode). Those mount paths are read-only: to change this context, edit this\\nfile (docs/AGENTS.md), never the mount paths.\\n\\\"Global\\\" agents, skills, MCP servers and policies below span two sources:\\nmanigot's own core content (from a located checkout when one exists, else\\nthe copy embedded in the installed binary), and catalog-installed content in\\nyour user data dir (`$MANIGOT_DATA_DIR`, else `$XDG_DATA_HOME/manigot`, else\\n`~/.local/share/manigot`), which overrides core content of the same name;\\nproject content in docs/ overrides both. Catalog content is installed on\\ndemand from the defaults catalog with `mg defaults install` (run on the host,\\nnever from inside a session); the core agents never come from there.\\nThe same global agents (from the checkout or the embedded copy — mounted read-only into\\nthe container at the CLI's global agent location, or delivered into the host\\nCLI's config for `mg host`: symlinked for Claude Code, converted for OpenCode)\\nare available under @name either way, and custom\\nproject agents in docs/agents/ work under both tools — write them in the\\nbuilt-in format (name:, description:, tools: Read, Grep, ...), no per-tool\\nformat needed. To make a custom agent read-only under OpenCode, add a\\n`permission:` frontmatter block (the built-in format manigot's conversion\\npasses through to OpenCode's schema — see the manigot README's agent section);\\nthe read-only built-in agents' blocks deny the destructive git commands\\n(worktree management, branch -d/-D, reset, checkout, push, ...).\\nGlobal skills (catalog-installed into your user data dir via\\n`mg defaults install` — the manigot checkout ships none; mounted read-only into\\nthe container at the CLI's global skills location, or delivered into the host\\nCLI's config for `mg host`: symlinked dirs for Claude Code, copied dirs for\\nOpenCode) are loaded by the CLI at startup and are available to every agent\\nand to agentless invocations; custom project skills in `docs/skills/`\\n(`<name>/SKILL.md`, overriding global skills of the same name) work under both\\ntools the same way — write them in the standard format (a directory with a\\nSKILL.md carrying name:/description: frontmatter, plus optional support\\nfiles), no per-tool format needed.\\nGlobal MCP (Model Context Protocol) server definitions (JSON files, one per\\nserver — catalog-installable into your user data dir via `mg defaults\\ninstall mcp/context7`; the manigot checkout ships none) are merged with any custom\\nproject servers in `docs/mcp/` (same filename overrides the global server\\nentirely, a new filename adds one) and delivered into every session: manigot\\nresolves `$VARNAME` tokens in each server's config against its own `.env`\\nhost-side, converts the merged, resolved set into whichever CLI is running,\\nand mounts it read-only — a generated `.mcp.json` for Claude Code, folded\\ninto OpenCode's config for OpenCode. A server definition never carries a\\nliteral secret; write one in the standard format (`{\\\"type\\\": \\\"http\\\", \\\"url\\\":\\n\\\"...\\\", \\\"headers\\\": {\\\"SOME_KEY\\\": \\\"$SOME_KEY\\\"}}` for a hosted server, or\\n`{\\\"type\\\": \\\"stdio\\\", \\\"command\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"args\\\": [...], \\\"env\\\": {\\\"SOME_KEY\\\":\\n\\\"$SOME_KEY\\\"}}` for a locally-spawned one), no per-tool format needed.\\nUnlike agents/skills (which `mg host` still delivers, via a symlink/copy\\ninto your own CLI config), `mg host` sessions don't deliver MCP servers at\\nall — they'd need to already be configured in your own CLI's own config.\\nPolicies (from manigot's global `policies/` — plain markdown files,\\none per file) are merged with any custom project policies in `docs/policies/`\\nthe same way (same filename overrides the global policy entirely, a new\\nfilename adds one), but unlike a skill a policy is *unconditionally\\ninjected* into this very context file rather than relevance-triggered — a\\nstanding rule like \\\"always use PSR-12 in this project\\\" must not depend on\\nthe agent deciding a skill's description matches. The merged policies are\\nconcatenated (sorted by filename, joined with a blank line) and appended\\nafter this file's own content when mounted; with no `docs/AGENTS.md`/\\n`CLAUDE.md` at all, the policies alone become the mounted context instead of\\nbeing silently dropped. No policies anywhere leaves the mount unchanged —\\nmanigot ships none by default (project/user taste, not a manigot-opinionated\\ndefault).\\nA system-wide meta prompt (from manigot's global `prompts/meta.md` — mounted\\nread-only into the container at each CLI's global instruction location\\n`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` for Claude Code, `~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md` for\\nOpenCode, or copied into the host CLI's own global instruction file for\\n`mg host`) is injected into every session above the agents, skills and this\\nproject context, together with any other `prompts/*.md` files layered after\\nit. This file still wins on conflict — it is the most\\nproject-specific layer.\\nCustom agents that must commit (like the built-in developer/reviewer, or\\nthe catalog agent quality, installed via `mg defaults`)\\ndeclare `commit: true` in their frontmatter; agents that never commit declare\\n`commit: false` and get a read-only git mount. The default — no agent named,\\nfile missing, or marker absent/unknown — is a writable git mount, so a\\ncommitting agent is never broken by a missing marker.\\nAgent files also carry a `tier:` marker: `tier: core` for the agents manigot\\nitself launches by name (analyst, developer, reviewer, chat, git-solver,\\nprompter — guaranteed present), `tier: catalog` for the rest (absent = catalog).\\nCore means guaranteed presence, not locked: a `docs/agents/<name>.md` override\\nstill replaces the content. Like `commit:`, it is stripped for OpenCode.\\nAgent files can also deny specific commands (`deny:` — a command deny-list)\\nand declare a network isolation level (`network:`); both are designed but not\\nyet enforced. `docs/agent-template.md` in the manigot checkout is a copy-me\\nreference agent showing the full frontmatter surface and every guardrail.\\nEvery job-worktree session ends with a host-side sweep-commit of leftover\\nchanges (a `[<id>] chore: commit all` commit), so read-only agents' outputs\\n(like the analyst's tasks.md) are committed after their session and `mg done`\\nis never blocked by uncommitted leftovers. The sweep never runs for plain\\nsessions, `mg host` sessions, or any `--job` resolution that stays on the main\\nworktree — the flat-scan fallback (a repo with no local branches, or a\\nnon-git project — there is no job worktree to sweep) and a pre-worktree job\\n(its branch still checked out in the main worktree itself) alike — since the\\nmain project root holds the user's own uncommitted work.\\nAgent sessions also restrict git to reading history and making commits (the\\nsession git shim): worktree management, branch deletes, resets, checkouts,\\npushes, and the other destructive subcommands are refused. The one exception\\nis @git-solver, the git expert, which gets the full local git toolbox (only\\nnetwork git — push/fetch/pull/remote — stays refused).\\nThe manigot TUI's job detail view also offers a `t` key that opens the job's\\nbranch diff in tig (`mg diff <job> --tig`) in a tmux split pane / new\\nterminal, gated on tig being installed on the host, and an `l` key that\\ntails the job's `session.log` — the verbose raw stream mg-jdi writes each\\ninvocation's output into as it happens, distinct from the log tab's\\n`run.log` event summary — live in a tmux split pane / new terminal (piped\\nthrough `jq` when available on the host, falling back to a plain `tail -f`\\notherwise), gated on a session.log existing for the job (created at mg-jdi\\nrun start). Capital `L` tails the same file in a condensed form instead\\n(`mg logtail`, same gate): reasoning/tool-call events collapsed to short\\nlabels or hidden entirely per a fixed classifier, with anything unrecognized\\nstill shown raw — `l`'s raw view stays reachable at all times, never\\nreplaced.\\n`mg serve` is the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a control API\\nover a registry of named project roots (an explicit config file,\\n`serve.json` in manigot's user config dir — `$MANIGOT_CONFIG_DIR`, else\\n`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/manigot`, else `~/.config/manigot` — holding `{\\\"projects\\\":\\n[{\\\"name\\\": \\\"my-project\\\", \\\"path\\\": \\\"/abs/root\\\"}, ...]}` — `name` is the required,\\noperator-chosen URL segment the project is served under; no scanning;\\nchange it with `mg serve-projects add/rm` (or `mg serve-project [name]`, a\\none-shot shortcut that registers the current directory and suggests its base\\nname as the project name) — or by editing — and restart), so\\nany surface — a web UI, a\\nnative GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client from localhost or a\\nVPS. It binds `127.0.0.1:18080` by default (tokenless —\\nthe machine's own user is the trust boundary); a non-loopback bind REQUIRES a\\nbearer token (`--token` or `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN`) or the daemon refuses to start,\\nand TLS is the reverse proxy's job (Caddy/nginx), never the daemon's. The v1\\nAPI is read-only (projects, jobs, job files, jdi status + logs, diff, agents,\\nhealth, project git status/log); job two added the mutating lifecycle (create\\njob, edit brief, launch one agent detached, launch mg jdi detached, done,\\ndelete, push, project git fetch/pull/push, prune, orphaned-worktree list + delete)\\nand a live session-log SSE stream\\n(`GET .../jobs/<j>/session-log/stream` — the network twin of the TUI's `l`\\ntail, resumable via `?from=<byte offset>`), with the detached launches\\nreturning 202 and the caller watching the job's session.log. The whole\\nsurface is a new trust boundary: URL segments are never joined into\\nfilesystem paths, credentials are never returned in any response, every\\nrequest is audit-logged (never the token), and the git-mutating operations\\n(create, edit-brief, done, delete, push, project git pull/push,\\norphan-delete) serialize per project root via the `serve.ProjectLocks`\\npattern — while launch-agent, launch-jdi,\\nprune, the profile-management routes (`GET/POST /profiles`, `POST\\n/profiles/{id}/delete`, `GET /providers`, `GET /models`) and the schedule\\nmanagement routes (`GET/POST /schedule`, `PUT /schedule/{id}`, `POST\\n/schedule/{id}/delete`, `/enable`, `/disable` — list/inspect/create/edit/\\nenable/disable/delete of a scheduled routine, definition management only)\\n— machine-global stores, like the global settings write — and every read\\nendpoint deliberately do NOT take the lock, so a job launch never waits\\nbehind an unrelated merge.\\nTwo API surfaces make a deliberate, bounded outbound network fetch:\\n`GET /models` browses the public models.dev catalog, fail-soft (a fetch\\nfailure answers `available: false`, never a 500), so the web UI's add-profile\\nwizard can offer a provider's known models; and `POST /projects/<p>/git/fetch`\\nrefreshes the project root's remote-tracking refs on demand — a fetch failure\\nsurfaces as a structured error carrying git's own message, never a silent\\nsuccess — so a commit pushed from another machine shows up as `behind` on the\\nGit view: the view paints the local status/log read (`GET\\n/projects/<p>/git/status` is purely local, never a network touch) instantly,\\ncalls this fetch in the background, then re-reads, and the Refresh button\\nre-checks the remote the same way. A fetch only moves `refs/remotes/*`, never\\nthe checked-out branch or working tree, so the local read stays instant.\\nThe token is provisioned out-of-band: `mg serve-token` generates a fresh one\\ninto the manigot secrets store (`manigot-secrets.json` in the same user\\nconfig dir, 0600) as\\n`MG_SERVE_TOKEN`, read once at daemon startup — the\\nAPI itself never issues, creates, or returns one.\\nCopying text from inside a session uses OSC 52: your terminal emulator must\\nsupport it, and tmux needs `set-clipboard on` when the session runs inside\\ntmux (mg forwards your terminal environment into the container and warns at\\nsession start when it detects tmux would swallow the clipboard writes — see\\nthe manigot README's \\\"Clipboard / copying from agent sessions\\\" section).\\nOne deliberate exception in that forwarding: OpenCode sessions never see\\n`TMUX`/`TMUX_PANE`, because OpenCode's TMUX-gated tmux DCS-passthrough OSC 52\\nform is discarded by default tmux config (`allow-passthrough` off) — stripping\\n`TMUX` makes it emit plain OSC 52, which tmux's `set-clipboard on` handles.\\nThe `shot` tool (`/usr/local/bin/shot`, see the manigot README's PLAYWRIGHT\\ndoc) renders a URL to a PNG + model-free render report. The developer agent\\nuses it to verify rendered work; read-only agents consume the artifacts and\\nnever run it.\\nClaude Code respects your host terminal's own color theme automatically;\\nOpenCode needs an explicit theme setting, which manigot exposes as a global\\nsetting shared across every OpenCode profile (`mg theme <name>`, or the\\nTUI's settings screen) — see the manigot README's Theme section. This is a\\nmanigot-level setting, not something to configure per-project here.\\nKeep this file tool-neutral — write it for \\\"the agent\\\", not for one vendor.\\n-->\\n\\nThis is the manigot catalog: a curated, git-tracked collection of portable agent\\ncontent — agent definitions, Agent Skills, an MCP server definition, and prompt\\nlayers — that manigot mounts into agent sessions. It is content, not an\\napplication: there is no source code, build, or test suite at the repository\\nroot. Work here means adding and refining agents and skills; the consumers are\\nthe agent sessions manigot runs, vendor-agnostically, against other projects.\\n\\n## Stack\\n- Content formats, not an app: Markdown agent definitions (`agents/*.md`),\\n  Agent Skills (`skills/*/SKILL.md`), JSON MCP server definitions\\n  (`mcp/*.json`), and Markdown prompt layers (`prompts/*.md`).\\n- Runtime: manigot — the same content is delivered to whichever CLI runs the\\n  session, so all definitions stay tool-neutral.\\n- Helper languages inside skills: Bash and Python 3. No application runtime.\\n- No package manager or manifest at the repo root. The only vendored tooling —\\n  the `skills-ref` validator under `skills/skill-authoring/spec/skills-ref/` —\\n  is a Python package managed with `uv`.\\n\\n## Architecture\\n- `agents/` — 17 agent definitions, one `<name>.md` each: YAML frontmatter\\n  (`name:`, `description:`, `tools:`, `commit:`, and a `permission:` block)\\n  plus the agent's role prompt. 6 declare `commit: true`; 11 declare\\n  `commit: false` (read-only). manigot launches these by name.\\n- `skills/` — 78 Agent Skills, one directory each. Required `SKILL.md` whose\\n  frontmatter is limited to `name:` and a one-line, trigger-rich `description:`;\\n  optional `references/`, `scripts/`, `assets/`, and `agents/` support dirs;\\n  vendored skills also carry `LICENSE.txt` + `PROVENANCE.md` recording the\\n  upstream and the adaptations made. `skill-authoring/` is the meta-skill: it\\n  holds `workflows/`, `templates/`, the format `spec/`, a vendored\\n  `spec/skills-ref/` validator, and `sources/`.\\n- `mcp/` — MCP server definitions, one JSON file per server, keyed by\\n  filename. `context7.json` is an HTTP server whose key is read from\\n  `$CONTEXT7_API_KEY`.\\n- `prompts/` — prompt layers injected above the agents: `meta.md` (the\\n  system-wide meta prompt) and `interactive.md` (the interactive-session\\n  addendum, which only reaches sessions with a human present).\\n- `docs/` — the project-context source: `AGENTS.md` (this file) and\\n  `CLAUDE.md`, plus an empty `docs/jobs/` for job workspaces.\\n- `.manigot/manigot.json` — manigot project config (`baseBranch: main`).\\n- Mount artifacts: `/workspace/AGENTS.md`, `.opencode/`, and `.claude/` are\\n  generated, git-excluded mirrors of `docs/`. They are read-only in a session\\n  and must never be edited directly.\\n\\n## Commands\\n- `./skills/skill-authoring/scripts/validate.sh <skill-dir>` — validate a\\n  skill's structure (requires `uv`; runs the vendored `skills-ref` validator).\\n- `skills-ref validate <skill-dir>` — the validator directly, after\\n  `pip install skills-ref` or via the vendored package.\\n- `uv run pytest` (from `skills/skill-authoring/spec/skills-ref/`) — run the\\n  vendored validator's test suite.\\n- `uv run ruff format .` / `uv run ruff check --fix .` (same directory) —\\n  format and lint the vendored validator.\\n- Repo-wide build / dev / test: none — this is a content repo.\\n  [add a command here if a root-level build or lint is ever introduced]\\n\\n## Hard rules\\n- Edit content in its source directories — `agents/`, `skills/`, `mcp/`,\\n  `prompts/` — and project context in `docs/`. Never edit the generated mount\\n  mirrors (`/workspace/AGENTS.md`, `.opencode/`, `.claude/`); changes there are\\n  lost.\\n- Keep everything tool-neutral. Write for \\\"the agent\\\", not one vendor, and add\\n  no per-vendor fields to shared definitions.\\n- Follow the frontmatter convention: skills use only `name:` + a one-line\\n  `description:` (no `license:`, `metadata:`, `allowed-tools:`, …); agents may\\n  set `tools:`, `commit:`, and `permission:`.\\n- `name:` must match the directory name, and `description:` must be\\n  trigger-rich — it is what decides whether an agent or skill ever loads.\\n- For vendored skills, keep `LICENSE.txt` and record the upstream, its license,\\n  and every adaptation in `PROVENANCE.md`.\\n- Validate a skill before committing changes to it.\\n- Keep changes scoped to the agent or skill you were asked to touch; do not\\n  rewrite unrelated definitions.\\n- NEVER modify files outside /workspace. When scope is unclear: ask, don't guess.\\n</system-reminder>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"# Brief: new policy: playwright only if necessary\\n\\ntype: feature\\nid: prior\\nbranch: feature/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary\\ndate: 2026-10-11\\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\\n\\n## What\\n\\nLet's create a new policy: only use playwright if strictly helpful. The agents get stuck a long time trying to playwright every possible thing instead of using easy paths of static analysis. Playwright can be helpful, but it costs and should only be used if really necessary/helpful.\\n\\n## Why\\n\\n<!-- Why does this need to exist? What problem does it solve for the user? -->\\n\\n## Out of scope\\n\\n<!-- What are we explicitly NOT doing in this job? -->\\n\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[\"/workspace/docs/AGENTS.md\"],\"display\":{\"type\":\"file\",\"path\":\"/workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/brief.md\",\"text\":\"# Brief: new policy: playwright only if necessary\\n\\ntype: feature\\nid: prior\\nbranch: feature/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary\\ndate: 2026-10-11\\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\\n\\n## What\\n\\nLet's create a new policy: only use playwright if strictly helpful. The agents get stuck a long time trying to playwright every possible thing instead of using easy paths of static analysis. Playwright can be helpful, but it costs and should only be used if really necessary/helpful.\\n\\n## Why\\n\\n<!-- Why does this need to exist? What problem does it solve for the user? -->\\n\\n## Out of scope\\n\\n<!-- What are we explicitly NOT doing in this job? -->\\n\\n## Notes\\n\\n<!-- Anything the analyst or developer should know before starting. -->\",\"lineStart\":1,\"lineEnd\":23,\"totalLines\":23,\"truncated\":false}},\"title\":\"docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/brief.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1791733791800,\"end\":1791733791886}},\"id\":\"prt_12ba7fbde0019ZECuDDTYeVUvS\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7f450001RWwpADlrw5uoec\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1791733792002,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_01_n1IQGbBpl1JjpeTkfUoV3771\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/tasks.md\"},\"output\":\"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/tasks.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: # Tasks: new policy: playwright only if necessary\\n2: \\n3: id: prior\\n4: analyst: analyst\\n5: date: 2026-10-11\\n6: \\n7: <!-- Produced by @analyst from brief.md. -->\\n8: \\n9: ## Overview\\n10: \\n11: Add one new global catalog policy under `policies/` capturing the brief's rule:\\n12: browser automation (Playwright, and the `shot` render path that drives it) is\\n13: expensive, so it should be reached for only when it is genuinely necessary or\\n14: helpful — static analysis and cheaper verification come first. This is a\\n15: content-only change: a single plain-markdown policy file, no frontmatter,\\n16: following the format the `medical_policies` job established (one-line heading +\\n17: the rule + at most a line or two of why; the filename is the identity).\\n18: \\n19: Scope boundaries confirmed from the brief:\\n20: - The deliverable is a **global catalog policy** at `","metadata":{"output":"...\n\ngents/developer.md`'s\\n  unconditional \\\"verify UI with shot\\\" wording), it is out of scope here and\\n  should be tracked as its own job.\\n\"},\"output\":\"Wrote file successfully.\",\"metadata\":{\"diagnostics\":{},\"filepath\":\"/workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/tasks.md\",\"exists\":true,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/tasks.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1791733774544,\"end\":1791733774563}},\"id\":\"prt_12ba79b83001l65PxXaCWNKehK\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba78cd7001pIoFXm6ssacJlY\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1791733774637,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba7b91d001JTkqUSSfHUfuT2\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"snapshot\":\"\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba78cd7001pIoFXm6ssacJlY\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":44239,\"input\":550,\"output\":1438,\"reasoning\":267,\"cache\":{\"write\":0,\"read\":41984}},\"cost\":0.001231452}}\n+{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1791733775804,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba7bdb9001rh5ObGbjcnKDZB\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7b92a001ni4rn5R9de6zNm\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"snapshot\":\"\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"text\",\"timestamp\":1791733780401,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba7c115001a9kkY8tSya2gug\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7b92a001ni4rn5R9de6zNm\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Done. I read `brief.md`, verified the branch, and wrote the task list to `tasks.md`.\\n\\n## Branch check\\n`brief.md` says `branch: feature/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary`; the worktree HEAD (`/workspace/.git` → worktree `.../prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/HEAD`) is `refs/heads/feature/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary`. They match — proceeding.\\n\\n## What I found\\n- This is the manigot-catalog content repo. Policies live in the top-level `policies/` (not `docs/policies/`, which is the per-project tier). Existing files: `ask-before-adding-dependencies.md`, `keep-it-committed.md`, `no-debug-leftovers.md`, `secrets-hygiene.md`.\\n- The archived `docs/jobs/archive/medical_policies/` job is the format precedent: plain markdown, no frontmatter, filename is identity, heading + rule + a line of why, and four decision criteria (universal, short always/never, no restating skills/agents, not situational).\\n- Relevant existing content the new policy must not contradict: `agents/developer.md` (~107–129) tells the developer to verify UI with `shot`; `agents/tester.md` (~96) points at the `webapp-testing` skill (Playwright); plus the `webapp-testing`/`frontend-design` skills and `prompts/meta.md`.\\n\\n## Task list (written to `tasks.md`)\\n\\n- **TASK-1 — Author `policies/playwright-only-if-necessary.md`.** Create the short, universal always-on policy: browser automation/Playwright is costly, so use it only when strictly necessary/helpful; prefer static analysis and cheaper checks. *Files:* new policy file. *Depends:* none. *Risk:* low — additive markdown; only judgement call is framing.\\n- **TASK-2 — Audit the new policy against the existing catalog.** Check it against the `medical_policies` criteria and existing agent/skill/meta-prompt content, shorten if needed, confirm it adds scope and does not contradict `developer.md`/`tester.md`, and record the reasoning in `implementation.md`. *Files:* policy file (may be edited), `implementation.md`. *Depends:* TASK-1. *Risk:* medium — judgement-based, can change/trim the wording.\\n\\n## Two things I flagged rather than guessed\\n1. **Framing vs. the criteria:** a rule named purely after one tool reads as *situational*, which the precedent says disqualifies a *global* policy. I resolved this conservatively toward the universal principle (prefer the cheapest sufficient verification; name Playwright as the example) and noted the alternative (a `docs/policies/` project rule or a skill) if the reviewer prefers it.\\n2. **Tension with `agents/developer.md`:** its unconditional \\\"verify UI changes with `shot`\\\" guidance conflicts with \\\"only use Playwright if necessary.\\\" I scoped the fix to wording inside the policy and explicitly kept editing `agents/` out of scope, to be flagged if it can't be reconciled in-file.\\n\\nThe brief's `Why`/`Out of scope`/`Notes` are empty, but the What is specific enough to proceed — no human decision needed. I did not commit (read-only agent).\",\"time\":{\"start\":1791733776661,\"end\":1791733780395}}}\n+{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1791733780436,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba7cfc2001cJoL6W2iC5TVvj\",\"reason\":\"stop\",\"snapshot\":\"\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7b92a001ni4rn5R9de6zNm\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed459432affebR6xC0AIZfMpXB\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":45039,\"input\":223,\"output\":735,\"reasoning\":49,\"cache\":{\"write\":0,\"read\":44032}},\"cost\":0.000635946}}\n+\n+=== 2026-10-11T17:49:40+02:00 developer (attempt 1) ===\n+{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1791733788280,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba7ee7300161OYW6EwiI9Jp3\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7e0dd001Zt6uQvxOzblyTf\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"snapshot\":\"\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1791733789690,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"bash\",\"callID\":\"call_00_GyLEKTlHCf2TyinCBxmZ6708\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"git branch --show-current && echo \\\"---\\\" && git status --short && echo \\\"---\\\" && ls /workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary\"},\"output\":\"feature/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary\\n---\\n M docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/session.log\\n?? docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/tokens.json\\n---\\nbrief.md\\nimplementation.md\\nsession.log\\ntasks.md\\ntokens.json\\nverdict.md\\n\",\"metadata\":{\"output\":\"feature/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary\\n---\\n M docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/session.log\\n?? docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/tokens.json\\n---\\nbrief.md\\nimplementation.md\\nsession.log\\ntasks.md\\ntokens.json\\nverdict.md\\n\",\"exit\":0,\"truncated\":false},\"title\":\"git branch --show-current && echo \\\"---\\\" && git status --short && echo \\\"---\\\" && ls /workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary\",\"time\":{\"start\":1791733789481,\"end\":1791733789653}},\"id\":\"prt_12ba7f287001VSo82t9dvmb05P\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7e0dd001Zt6uQvxOzblyTf\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"step_finish\",\"timestamp\":1791733789785,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba7f440001Al6yyfAF3r5GCq\",\"reason\":\"tool-calls\",\"snapshot\":\"\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7e0dd001Zt6uQvxOzblyTf\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"type\":\"step-finish\",\"tokens\":{\"total\":19438,\"input\":19347,\"output\":75,\"reasoning\":16,\"cache\":{\"write\":0,\"read\":0}},\"cost\":0.00295665}}\n+{\"type\":\"step_start\",\"timestamp\":1791733791004,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"part\":{\"id\":\"prt_12ba7f917001yS2YJteumnLZuz\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7f450001RWwpADlrw5uoec\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"snapshot\":\"\",\"type\":\"step-start\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1791733791892,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_00_qg9ldgBgjodg434GUr8y6009\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/brief.md\"},\"output\":\"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/brief.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: # Brief: new policy: playwright only if necessary\\n2: \\n3: type: feature\\n4: id: prior\\n5: branch: feature/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary\\n6: date: 2026-10-11\\n7: author: Leander Muskalla\\n8: \\n9: ## What\\n10: \\n11: Let's create a new policy: only use playwright if strictly helpful. The agents get stuck a long time trying to playwright every possible thing instead of using easy paths of static analysis. Playwright can be helpful, but it costs and should only be used if really necessary/helpful.\\n12: \\n13: ## Why\\n14: \\n15: <!-- Why does this need to exist? What problem does it solve for the user? -->\\n16: \\n17: ## Out of scope\\n18: \\n19: <!-- What are we explicitly NOT doing in this job? -->\\n20: \\n21: ## Notes\\n22: \\n23: <!-- Anything the analyst or developer should know before starting. -->\\n\\n(End of file - total 23 lines)\\n</content>\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nInstructions from: /workspace/docs/AGENTS.md\\n# manigot-catalog\\n\\n<!--\\nThis is your project context, loaded by the agent at the start of every session.\\nmanigot is vendor-agnostic: it runs Claude Code or OpenCode against the same\\nproject (`mg --profile claude-pro` vs `mg --profile zai`/`--profile\\nopencode-go`/`--profile opencode-zen`/`--profile opencode-zen-free`/`--profile\\ndeepseek-direct`), and this one file serves both — manigot mounts it read-only\\nwherever the selected tool looks for it\\n(/workspace/AGENTS.md for OpenCode, /workspace/.claude/CLAUDE.md for Claude\\nCode). Those mount paths are read-only: to change this context, edit this\\nfile (docs/AGENTS.md), never the mount paths.\\n\\\"Global\\\" agents, skills, MCP servers and policies below span two sources:\\nmanigot's own core content (from a located checkout when one exists, else\\nthe copy embedded in the installed binary), and catalog-installed content in\\nyour user data dir (`$MANIGOT_DATA_DIR`, else `$XDG_DATA_HOME/manigot`, else\\n`~/.local/share/manigot`), which overrides core content of the same name;\\nproject content in docs/ overrides both. Catalog content is installed on\\ndemand from the defaults catalog with `mg defaults install` (run on the host,\\nnever from inside a session); the core agents never come from there.\\nThe same global agents (from the checkout or the embedded copy — mounted read-only into\\nthe container at the CLI's global agent location, or delivered into the host\\nCLI's config for `mg host`: symlinked for Claude Code, converted for OpenCode)\\nare available under @name either way, and custom\\nproject agents in docs/agents/ work under both tools — write them in the\\nbuilt-in format (name:, description:, tools: Read, Grep, ...), no per-tool\\nformat needed. To make a custom agent read-only under OpenCode, add a\\n`permission:` frontmatter block (the built-in format manigot's conversion\\npasses through to OpenCode's schema — see the manigot README's agent section);\\nthe read-only built-in agents' blocks deny the destructive git commands\\n(worktree management, branch -d/-D, reset, checkout, push, ...).\\nGlobal skills (catalog-installed into your user data dir via\\n`mg defaults install` — the manigot checkout ships none; mounted read-only into\\nthe container at the CLI's global skills location, or delivered into the host\\nCLI's config for `mg host`: symlinked dirs for Claude Code, copied dirs for\\nOpenCode) are loaded by the CLI at startup and are available to every agent\\nand to agentless invocations; custom project skills in `docs/skills/`\\n(`<name>/SKILL.md`, overriding global skills of the same name) work under both\\ntools the same way — write them in the standard format (a directory with a\\nSKILL.md carrying name:/description: frontmatter, plus optional support\\nfiles), no per-tool format needed.\\nGlobal MCP (Model Context Protocol) server definitions (JSON files, one per\\nserver — catalog-installable into your user data dir via `mg defaults\\ninstall mcp/context7`; the manigot checkout ships none) are merged with any custom\\nproject servers in `docs/mcp/` (same filename overrides the global server\\nentirely, a new filename adds one) and delivered into every session: manigot\\nresolves `$VARNAME` tokens in each server's config against its own `.env`\\nhost-side, converts the merged, resolved set into whichever CLI is running,\\nand mounts it read-only — a generated `.mcp.json` for Claude Code, folded\\ninto OpenCode's config for OpenCode. A server definition never carries a\\nliteral secret; write one in the standard format (`{\\\"type\\\": \\\"http\\\", \\\"url\\\":\\n\\\"...\\\", \\\"headers\\\": {\\\"SOME_KEY\\\": \\\"$SOME_KEY\\\"}}` for a hosted server, or\\n`{\\\"type\\\": \\\"stdio\\\", \\\"command\\\": \\\"...\\\", \\\"args\\\": [...], \\\"env\\\": {\\\"SOME_KEY\\\":\\n\\\"$SOME_KEY\\\"}}` for a locally-spawned one), no per-tool format needed.\\nUnlike agents/skills (which `mg host` still delivers, via a symlink/copy\\ninto your own CLI config), `mg host` sessions don't deliver MCP servers at\\nall — they'd need to already be configured in your own CLI's own config.\\nPolicies (from manigot's global `policies/` — plain markdown files,\\none per file) are merged with any custom project policies in `docs/policies/`\\nthe same way (same filename overrides the global policy entirely, a new\\nfilename adds one), but unlike a skill a policy is *unconditionally\\ninjected* into this very context file rather than relevance-triggered — a\\nstanding rule like \\\"always use PSR-12 in this project\\\" must not depend on\\nthe agent deciding a skill's description matches. The merged policies are\\nconcatenated (sorted by filename, joined with a blank line) and appended\\nafter this file's own content when mounted; with no `docs/AGENTS.md`/\\n`CLAUDE.md` at all, the policies alone become the mounted context instead of\\nbeing silently dropped. No policies anywhere leaves the mount unchanged —\\nmanigot ships none by default (project/user taste, not a manigot-opinionated\\ndefault).\\nA system-wide meta prompt (from manigot's global `prompts/meta.md` — mounted\\nread-only into the container at each CLI's global instruction location\\n`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` for Claude Code, `~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md` for\\nOpenCode, or copied into the host CLI's own global instruction file for\\n`mg host`) is injected into every session above the agents, skills and this\\nproject context, together with any other `prompts/*.md` files layered after\\nit. This file still wins on conflict — it is the most\\nproject-specific layer.\\nCustom agents that must commit (like the built-in developer/reviewer, or\\nthe catalog agent quality, installed via `mg defaults`)\\ndeclare `commit: true` in their frontmatter; agents that never commit declare\\n`commit: false` and get a read-only git mount. The default — no agent named,\\nfile missing, or marker absent/unknown — is a writable git mount, so a\\ncommitting agent is never broken by a missing marker.\\nAgent files also carry a `tier:` marker: `tier: core` for the agents manigot\\nitself launches by name (analyst, developer, reviewer, chat, git-solver,\\nprompter — guaranteed present), `tier: catalog` for the rest (absent = catalog).\\nCore means guaranteed presence, not locked: a `docs/agents/<name>.md` override\\nstill replaces the content. Like `commit:`, it is stripped for OpenCode.\\nAgent files can also deny specific commands (`deny:` — a command deny-list)\\nand declare a network isolation level (`network:`); both are designed but not\\nyet enforced. `docs/agent-template.md` in the manigot checkout is a copy-me\\nreference agent showing the full frontmatter surface and every guardrail.\\nEvery job-worktree session ends with a host-side sweep-commit of leftover\\nchanges (a `[<id>] chore: commit all` commit), so read-only agents' outputs\\n(like the analyst's tasks.md) are committed after their session and `mg done`\\nis never blocked by uncommitted leftovers. The sweep never runs for plain\\nsessions, `mg host` sessions, or any `--job` resolution that stays on the main\\nworktree — the flat-scan fallback (a repo with no local branches, or a\\nnon-git project — there is no job worktree to sweep) and a pre-worktree job\\n(its branch still checked out in the main worktree itself) alike — since the\\nmain project root holds the user's own uncommitted work.\\nAgent sessions also restrict git to reading history and making commits (the\\nsession git shim): worktree management, branch deletes, resets, checkouts,\\npushes, and the other destructive subcommands are refused. The one exception\\nis @git-solver, the git expert, which gets the full local git toolbox (only\\nnetwork git — push/fetch/pull/remote — stays refused).\\nThe manigot TUI's job detail view also offers a `t` key that opens the job's\\nbranch diff in tig (`mg diff <job> --tig`) in a tmux split pane / new\\nterminal, gated on tig being installed on the host, and an `l` key that\\ntails the job's `session.log` — the verbose raw stream mg-jdi writes each\\ninvocation's output into as it happens, distinct from the log tab's\\n`run.log` event summary — live in a tmux split pane / new terminal (piped\\nthrough `jq` when available on the host, falling back to a plain `tail -f`\\notherwise), gated on a session.log existing for the job (created at mg-jdi\\nrun start). Capital `L` tails the same file in a condensed form instead\\n(`mg logtail`, same gate): reasoning/tool-call events collapsed to short\\nlabels or hidden entirely per a fixed classifier, with anything unrecognized\\nstill shown raw — `l`'s raw view stays reachable at all times, never\\nreplaced.\\n`mg serve` is the listener: a long-running daemon exposing a control API\\nover a registry of named project roots (an explicit config file,\\n`serve.json` in manigot's user config dir — `$MANIGOT_CONFIG_DIR`, else\\n`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/manigot`, else `~/.config/manigot` — holding `{\\\"projects\\\":\\n[{\\\"name\\\": \\\"my-project\\\", \\\"path\\\": \\\"/abs/root\\\"}, ...]}` — `name` is the required,\\noperator-chosen URL segment the project is served under; no scanning;\\nchange it with `mg serve-projects add/rm` (or `mg serve-project [name]`, a\\none-shot shortcut that registers the current directory and suggests its base\\nname as the project name) — or by editing — and restart), so\\nany surface — a web UI, a\\nnative GUI, a future CLI — can attach to it as a client from localhost or a\\nVPS. It binds `127.0.0.1:18080` by default (tokenless —\\nthe machine's own user is the trust boundary); a non-loopback bind REQUIRES a\\nbearer token (`--token` or `$MG_SERVE_TOKEN`) or the daemon refuses to start,\\nand TLS is the reverse proxy's job (Caddy/nginx), never the daemon's. The v1\\nAPI is read-only (projects, jobs, job files, jdi status + logs, diff, agents,\\nhealth, project git status/log); job two added the mutating lifecycle (create\\njob, edit brief, launch one agent detached, launch mg jdi detached, done,\\ndelete, push, project git fetch/pull/push, prune, orphaned-worktree list + delete)\\nand a live session-log SSE stream\\n(`GET .../jobs/<j>/session-log/stream` — the network twin of the TUI's `l`\\ntail, resumable via `?from=<byte offset>`), with the detached launches\\nreturning 202 and the caller watching the job's session.log. The whole\\nsurface is a new trust boundary: URL segments are never joined into\\nfilesystem paths, credentials are never returned in any response, every\\nrequest is audit-logged (never the token), and the git-mutating operations\\n(create, edit-brief, done, delete, push, project git pull/push,\\norphan-delete) serialize per project root via the `serve.ProjectLocks`\\npattern — while launch-agent, launch-jdi,\\nprune, the profile-management routes (`GET/POST /profiles`, `POST\\n/profiles/{id}/delete`, `GET /providers`, `GET /models`) and the schedule\\nmanagement routes (`GET/POST /schedule`, `PUT /schedule/{id}`, `POST\\n/schedule/{id}/delete`, `/enable`, `/disable` — list/inspect/create/edit/\\nenable/disable/delete of a scheduled routine, definition management only)\\n— machine-global stores, like the global settings write — and every read\\nendpoint deliberately do NOT take the lock, so a job launch never waits\\nbehind an unrelated merge.\\nTwo API surfaces make a deliberate, bounded outbound network fetch:\\n`GET /models` browses the public models.dev catalog, fail-soft (a fetch\\nfailure answers `available: false`, never a 500), so the web UI's add-profile\\nwizard can offer a provider's known models; and `POST /projects/<p>/git/fetch`\\nrefreshes the project root's remote-tracking refs on demand — a fetch failure\\nsurfaces as a structured error carrying git's own message, never a silent\\nsuccess — so a commit pushed from another machine shows up as `behind` on the\\nGit view: the view paints the local status/log read (`GET\\n/projects/<p>/git/status` is purely local, never a network touch) instantly,\\ncalls this fetch in the background, then re-reads, and the Refresh button\\nre-checks the remote the same way. A fetch only moves `refs/remotes/*`, never\\nthe checked-out branch or working tree, so the local read stays instant.\\nThe token is provisioned out-of-band: `mg serve-token` generates a fresh one\\ninto the manigot secrets store (`manigot-secrets.json` in the same user\\nconfig dir, 0600) as\\n`MG_SERVE_TOKEN`, read once at daemon startup — the\\nAPI itself never issues, creates, or returns one.\\nCopying text from inside a session uses OSC 52: your terminal emulator must\\nsupport it, and tmux needs `set-clipboard on` when the session runs inside\\ntmux (mg forwards your terminal environment into the container and warns at\\nsession start when it detects tmux would swallow the clipboard writes — see\\nthe manigot README's \\\"Clipboard / copying from agent sessions\\\" section).\\nOne deliberate exception in that forwarding: OpenCode sessions never see\\n`TMUX`/`TMUX_PANE`, because OpenCode's TMUX-gated tmux DCS-passthrough OSC 52\\nform is discarded by default tmux config (`allow-passthrough` off) — stripping\\n`TMUX` makes it emit plain OSC 52, which tmux's `set-clipboard on` handles.\\nThe `shot` tool (`/usr/local/bin/shot`, see the manigot README's PLAYWRIGHT\\ndoc) renders a URL to a PNG + model-free render report. The developer agent\\nuses it to verify rendered work; read-only agents consume the artifacts and\\nnever run it.\\nClaude Code respects your host terminal's own color theme automatically;\\nOpenCode needs an explicit theme setting, which manigot exposes as a global\\nsetting shared across every OpenCode profile (`mg theme <name>`, or the\\nTUI's settings screen) — see the manigot README's Theme section. This is a\\nmanigot-level setting, not something to configure per-project here.\\nKeep this file tool-neutral — write it for \\\"the agent\\\", not for one vendor.\\n-->\\n\\nThis is the manigot catalog: a curated, git-tracked collection of portable agent\\ncontent — agent definitions, Agent Skills, an MCP server definition, and prompt\\nlayers — that manigot mounts into agent sessions. It is content, not an\\napplication: there is no source code, build, or test suite at the repository\\nroot. Work here means adding and refining agents and skills; the consumers are\\nthe agent sessions manigot runs, vendor-agnostically, against other projects.\\n\\n## Stack\\n- Content formats, not an app: Markdown agent definitions (`agents/*.md`),\\n  Agent Skills (`skills/*/SKILL.md`), JSON MCP server definitions\\n  (`mcp/*.json`), and Markdown prompt layers (`prompts/*.md`).\\n- Runtime: manigot — the same content is delivered to whichever CLI runs the\\n  session, so all definitions stay tool-neutral.\\n- Helper languages inside skills: Bash and Python 3. No application runtime.\\n- No package manager or manifest at the repo root. The only vendored tooling —\\n  the `skills-ref` validator under `skills/skill-authoring/spec/skills-ref/` —\\n  is a Python package managed with `uv`.\\n\\n## Architecture\\n- `agents/` — 17 agent definitions, one `<name>.md` each: YAML frontmatter\\n  (`name:`, `description:`, `tools:`, `commit:`, and a `permission:` block)\\n  plus the agent's role prompt. 6 declare `commit: true`; 11 declare\\n  `commit: false` (read-only). manigot launches these by name.\\n- `skills/` — 78 Agent Skills, one directory each. Required `SKILL.md` whose\\n  frontmatter is limited to `name:` and a one-line, trigger-rich `description:`;\\n  optional `references/`, `scripts/`, `assets/`, and `agents/` support dirs;\\n  vendored skills also carry `LICENSE.txt` + `PROVENANCE.md` recording the\\n  upstream and the adaptations made. `skill-authoring/` is the meta-skill: it\\n  holds `workflows/`, `templates/`, the format `spec/`, a vendored\\n  `spec/skills-ref/` validator, and `sources/`.\\n- `mcp/` — MCP server definitions, one JSON file per server, keyed by\\n  filename. `context7.json` is an HTTP server whose key is read from\\n  `$CONTEXT7_API_KEY`.\\n- `prompts/` — prompt layers injected above the agents: `meta.md` (the\\n  system-wide meta prompt) and `interactive.md` (the interactive-session\\n  addendum, which only reaches sessions with a human present).\\n- `docs/` — the project-context source: `AGENTS.md` (this file) and\\n  `CLAUDE.md`, plus an empty `docs/jobs/` for job workspaces.\\n- `.manigot/manigot.json` — manigot project config (`baseBranch: main`).\\n- Mount artifacts: `/workspace/AGENTS.md`, `.opencode/`, and `.claude/` are\\n  generated, git-excluded mirrors of `docs/`. They are read-only in a session\\n  and must never be edited directly.\\n\\n## Commands\\n- `./skills/skill-authoring/scripts/validate.sh <skill-dir>` — validate a\\n  skill's structure (requires `uv`; runs the vendored `skills-ref` validator).\\n- `skills-ref validate <skill-dir>` — the validator directly, after\\n  `pip install skills-ref` or via the vendored package.\\n- `uv run pytest` (from `skills/skill-authoring/spec/skills-ref/`) — run the\\n  vendored validator's test suite.\\n- `uv run ruff format .` / `uv run ruff check --fix .` (same directory) —\\n  format and lint the vendored validator.\\n- Repo-wide build / dev / test: none — this is a content repo.\\n  [add a command here if a root-level build or lint is ever introduced]\\n\\n## Hard rules\\n- Edit content in its source directories — `agents/`, `skills/`, `mcp/`,\\n  `prompts/` — and project context in `docs/`. Never edit the generated mount\\n  mirrors (`/workspace/AGENTS.md`, `.opencode/`, `.claude/`); changes there are\\n  lost.\\n- Keep everything tool-neutral. Write for \\\"the agent\\\", not one vendor, and add\\n  no per-vendor fields to shared definitions.\\n- Follow the frontmatter convention: skills use only `name:` + a one-line\\n  `description:` (no `license:`, `metadata:`, `allowed-tools:`, …); agents may\\n  set `tools:`, `commit:`, and `permission:`.\\n- `name:` must match the directory name, and `description:` must be\\n  trigger-rich — it is what decides whether an agent or skill ever loads.\\n- For vendored skills, keep `LICENSE.txt` and record the upstream, its license,\\n  and every adaptation in `PROVENANCE.md`.\\n- Validate a skill before committing changes to it.\\n- Keep changes scoped to the agent or skill you were asked to touch; do not\\n  rewrite unrelated definitions.\\n- NEVER modify files outside /workspace. When scope is unclear: ask, don't guess.\\n</system-reminder>\",\"metadata\":{\"preview\":\"# Brief: new policy: playwright only if necessary\\n\\ntype: feature\\nid: prior\\nbranch: feature/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary\\ndate: 2026-10-11\\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\\n\\n## What\\n\\nLet's create a new policy: only use playwright if strictly helpful. The agents get stuck a long time trying to playwright every possible thing instead of using easy paths of static analysis. Playwright can be helpful, but it costs and should only be used if really necessary/helpful.\\n\\n## Why\\n\\n<!-- Why does this need to exist? What problem does it solve for the user? -->\\n\\n## Out of scope\\n\\n<!-- What are we explicitly NOT doing in this job? -->\\n\",\"truncated\":false,\"loaded\":[\"/workspace/docs/AGENTS.md\"],\"display\":{\"type\":\"file\",\"path\":\"/workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/brief.md\",\"text\":\"# Brief: new policy: playwright only if necessary\\n\\ntype: feature\\nid: prior\\nbranch: feature/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary\\ndate: 2026-10-11\\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\\n\\n## What\\n\\nLet's create a new policy: only use playwright if strictly helpful. The agents get stuck a long time trying to playwright every possible thing instead of using easy paths of static analysis. Playwright can be helpful, but it costs and should only be used if really necessary/helpful.\\n\\n## Why\\n\\n<!-- Why does this need to exist? What problem does it solve for the user? -->\\n\\n## Out of scope\\n\\n<!-- What are we explicitly NOT doing in this job? -->\\n\\n## Notes\\n\\n<!-- Anything the analyst or developer should know before starting. -->\",\"lineStart\":1,\"lineEnd\":23,\"totalLines\":23,\"truncated\":false}},\"title\":\"docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/brief.md\",\"time\":{\"start\":1791733791800,\"end\":1791733791886}},\"id\":\"prt_12ba7fbde0019ZECuDDTYeVUvS\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"messageID\":\"msg_12ba7f450001RWwpADlrw5uoec\"}}\n+{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1791733792002,\"sessionID\":\"ses_ed458233dffes0Lk4CTNEuavw6\",\"part\":{\"type\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"callID\":\"call_01_n1IQGbBpl1JjpeTkfUoV3771\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"filePath\":\"/workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/tasks.md\"},\"output\":\"<path>/workspace/docs/jobs/prior_new-policy-playwright-only-if-necessary/tasks.md</path>\\n<type>file</type>\\n<content>\\n1: # Tasks: new policy: playwright only if necessary\\n2: \\n3: id: prior\\n4: analyst: analyst\\n5: date: 2026-10-11\\n6: \\n7: <!-- Produced by @analyst from brief.md. -->\\n8: \\n9: ## Overview\\n10: \\n11: Add one new global catalog policy under `policies/` capturing the brief's rule:\\n12: browser automation (Playwright, and the `shot` render path that drives it) is\\n13: expensive, so it should be reached for only when it is genuinely necessary or\\n14: helpful — static analysis and cheaper verification come first. This is a\\n15: content-only change: a single plain-markdown policy file, no frontmatter,\\n16: following the format the `medical_policies` job established (one-line heading +\\n17: the rule + at most a line or two of why; the filename is the identity).\\n18: \\n19: Scope boundaries confirmed from the brief:\\n20: - The deliverable is a **global catalog policy** at `","exit":0,"truncated":true,"outputPath":"/home/claude/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_12baaa0ee001XK4t9uxEiOXoMy"},"title":"git diff main...HEAD","time":{"start":1791733964978,"end":1791733965054}},"id":"prt_12baa9fc8001WxqYPfvwpXuBU2","sessionID":"ses_ed455c351ffePunCilhy6Dmx21","messageID":"msg_12baa767e001OFuTosegd77lQ5"}}
